title: 'The file cache never expires: the timestamp is written but never evaluated'
updated: 2026-10-25T05:50:02Z
---

`WWW::ARDB::Cache::set` writes a `timestamp` into every cache file:

    { timestamp => time(), endpoint => ..., data => ... }

Nothing ever reads it. `Cache::get` returns the stored data as long as the file exists, so a response from weeks ago is served indefinitely — for the items list that means stale prices, stale recipes and stale loot tables for anyone using the library as a dependency.

The cache can only be emptied by an explicit `clear_cache` call, and `WWW::ARDB::CLI` exposes no flag for it (it offers `--no-cache`, which bypasses the cache entirely instead of refreshing it).

What to do:
1. Decide on and implement a TTL: read the stored `timestamp`, compare it against the configured maximum age, and treat an expired entry as a miss (fetch, then write again).
2. Make the maximum age configurable — an attribute on `WWW::ARDB::Cache` with a sensible default — and let `WWW::ARDB` pass it through, so a caller who wants a fresh response can ask for one without clearing the whole cache.
3. A cache entry without a usable timestamp (older file, hand-edited, truncated) must be treated as expired, not as a hit.
4. Write tests that pin the behaviour: an entry inside the TTL is a hit, an entry outside is a miss, and the file is rewritten after the refresh. Use the existing cache test file and a temporary cache directory; no sleeps longer than a second — write the timestamp directly into the file instead of waiting.
5. Keep the cache key and the file layout unchanged: `<namespace>_<endpoint>` plus `_` plus md5 of the JSON-encoded parameters, file `<key>.json`.

This changes what callers see, so it needs a `Changes` entry — describe the effect ("cached responses expire after N seconds instead of never"), not the implementation.

